      A `StatedClaim`, para a mesma escolha onde nada calcula o valor; a `Identity`, para o que
      calcula este.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="claim"      type="pm:Claim"/>
      <xs:element name="derivation" type="pm:FactorDerivation"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"     type="pm:ClaimAbsence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedAmountOrigin">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation xml:lang="en">
      # What it is

      Who could have held a different amount, or why nobody can say. It is `StatedConstraintOrigin`
      for `Nameplate/amountOrigin`, with two of its answers removed.

      # What it contains

      - `origin intrinsic/contractual/policy`: somebody can move this, and here is who.
      - `absent/reason = unmeasured`: nobody has asked who could hold a different number.
      - `absent/reason = notApplicable`: there is no committed amount to have an origin.

      # What it does not admit

      - `none`. An amount that was committed has an author, and where the number is fixed by the
        nature of the thing, `intrinsic` says so; `none` would be a second way to say it. A bound
        is different, which is why `Claim/boundOrigin` keeps the whole `StatedConstraintOrigin`.
      - A derivation. Nothing in the model computes who committed an amount.

      # See also

      `StatedConstraintOrigin` for the question and why it is wrapped. `ConstraintOrigin` for the
      three members.
    </xs:documentation><xs:documentation xml:lang="pt">
      **Português.**

      # O que é

      Diz quem poderia ter tido uma quantidade diferente, ou porque é que ninguém o sabe dizer; é
      o `StatedConstraintOrigin` do `Nameplate/amountOrigin`, com duas das suas respostas a menos.

      # O que contém

      - `origin intrinsic/contractual/policy`: alguém pode mudar isto, e diz-se quem.
      - `absent/reason = unmeasured`: ninguém perguntou quem poderia ter outro número.
      - `absent/reason = notApplicable`: não há quantidade comprometida que possa ter origem.

      # O que não admite

      - O `none`. Uma quantidade com que alguém se comprometeu tem autor, e onde o número vem da
        natureza da coisa, é o `intrinsic` que o diz, pelo que o `none` seria outra maneira de
        dizer o mesmo; já com um limite é diferente, e é por isso que o `Claim/boundOrigin`
        mantém o `StatedConstraintOrigin` inteiro.
      - Uma derivação, porque nada no modelo calcula quem se comprometeu com uma quantidade.

      # Ver também

      O `StatedConstraintOrigin`, para a pergunta e para a razão de vir envolvida; a
      `ConstraintOrigin`, para os três membros.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="origin" type="pm:ConstraintOrigin"/>
      <xs:element name="absent" type="pm:ClaimAbsence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>


  <!-- ==================================================================
       Stated: a value, or a typed reason there is none, and never silence.

       XSD 1.0 has no generics, so each stated type has a wrapper of its own.
       The length of the schema is what that guarantee costs.

       A wrapper exists only where a sender may have no value, and leaving one
       out says that whoever sends always knows the value. Nameplate and Jagged
       are not wrapped: their own quantities are, and wrapping the container as
       well would give one fact two places. The test is about every sender who
       might file, not about a sender who has the fact, which is why `sign` and
       `divisibility` are wrapped.

       A repeating element needs a wrapper more than a single one does. An
       optional repeating element looks as if it already handles absence, since
       a sender with nothing writes nothing, and that is the failure: an empty
       list is a blank field in the plural. It cannot tell apart

         somebody looked and there are none        `none`
         nobody looked                             `unmeasured`
         this document is the wrong place to ask   `notApplicable`

       For the couplings of a `Stack` and the eliminations of an `asrt:Fusion`,
       the two lists that could show the model wrong, the first two readings
       are opposite verdicts on the model. So `StatedCouplings` and
       `asrt:StatedEliminations` hold either a list with at least one member or
       an absence, and an empty list cannot be written.

       A borrowed term is wrapped where telling `none` from `unmeasured` matters
       at that position and no enclosing absence already says it:
       `Remainder/absorber`, `Provenance/standing`, `Regime/framework` and
       `Regime/chart`. A regime cannot be declined with a reason, only left
       out, and a filing may have several, so wrapping the whole regime would
       give a list of maybe-regimes, which says less than a plain list. A
       borrowed term is not wrapped where an enclosing wrapper already carries
       the absence (`borrowed` under `StatedBasis`, `holds` beside `cannotAsk`),
       or where the term adds to a fact rather than framing it
       (`asrt:Citation/instrument`).

       `divisibility` is wrapped for one more reason: it is asked of subjects
       that are not supplies at all, where nothing says the sender knows
       whether the thing comes in whole units. See `StatedDivisibility`.
